 favoring Democrats in an even-PVI district", "Prediction markets price Bennett around 82-83% to win", "Union endorsements flipping to Bennett and reported GOP concern about Kean", "Kean's incumbency and large cash-on-hand advantage partially offset the environment"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Democratic-leaning race: an even-PVI seat in a favorable environment, a September poll showing Bennett up five, and markets pricing her near 82%. Kean's incumbency and fundraising edge keep this from Likely D, but the weight of expert ratings and outside signals favors a Bennett flip.", "watch": "A late shift in the national environment or Kean consolidating Republican-leaning voters could tighten this back toward a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. (R) is running."]}, "overview": "New Jersey's 7th District is an even partisan-lean seat where Republican incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. faces a strong challenge from Democrat Rebecca Bennett. The national environment favors Democrats by several points, and most expert raters now call the race a Democratic flip, ranging from Tilt D to Likely D. A September live-caller poll of likely voters showed Bennett ahead 47-42, and prediction markets price her chances around 82-83%.\n\nKean retains real advantages: incumbency, a large cash-on-hand lead, and modest outside spending against Bennett. But union groups including firefighters and AFSCME have backed Bennett, and local reporting notes concern among some New Jersey Republicans about Kean's standing. The race remains competitive, with Bennett holding a modest but meaningful edge heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8072, "margin": 5.29}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8017, "margin": 5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8142, "margin": 5.5}]}